<Base title="About" description="About Talha M Blog">
  <section class="article-head"><h1>About</h1></section>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Welcome to Talha M Blog. This is a news and information website where every story is explained in simple, clear English, so that anyone — a student, a shopkeeper, a busy office worker, or a reader whose first language is not English — can understand the news without struggling with hard words. News moves fast and is often written in a complicated way. We believe that understanding what is happening in the world should not depend on how strong your English is. That is the whole idea behind this website.</p>

    <p>Every post on Talha M Blog tries to answer a few simple questions honestly: What happened? When and where did it happen? Who is involved? And why does it matter to ordinary people? We write in our own words, in short clear paragraphs, and we try to give the background of a story as well as the latest update, so that a reader who is hearing about a topic for the very first time can still follow it from start to finish.</p>

    <h2>What we write about</h2>
    <p>Our stories are organised into categories, so you can read only what interests you:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/category/world-news/">World News</a>: the biggest stories from around the world — major events, discoveries, disasters, science and remarkable places, explained step by step.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/us-news/">US News</a>: important stories from the United States — big companies, famous cases, jobs, technology giants and events that affect the whole world.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/pakistan-news/">Pakistan News</a>: politics, government decisions and important events inside Pakistan.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/celebrity/">Celebrity</a>: news and life stories of famous people — actors, singers, athletes and public figures — including their background and family, written with respect.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/ai/">AI</a>: new AI tools, AI companies and artificial intelligence news, explained simply for beginners.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/sports/">Sports</a>: cricket, football and other sports news, records and stories.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/technology/">Technology</a>: phones, apps, companies and the technology people use every day.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How we prepare a story</h2>
    <p>Before a story is published, we check the facts against reliable news sources. For important stories, we compare reports from more than one source, because a single report can be wrong, incomplete, or later corrected by the people involved. We write every post ourselves, in our own words. We do not copy and paste articles from other websites, and we do not publish rumours as if they were confirmed news. Where a report is not yet confirmed, we say so clearly inside the story, instead of hiding the doubt.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Every post shows the date and time it was published, in Pakistan time (PKT).</li>
      <li>When a story develops, we update the post and the page shows an "Updated" date.</li>
      <li>A green dot beside a post means it was published in the last 48 hours.</li>
      <li>Where possible, we name or list our sources, so you can read the original reports yourself.</li>
      <li>The pictures on this website are illustrations made for our stories, so readers should not mistake them for real photographs of an event.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Our promise about honesty</h2>
    <p>Trust is the most valuable thing a news website has. It is earned slowly and lost quickly, so we follow some simple rules. We never invent quotations and put them in someone's mouth. We never make up numbers, statistics or earnings. If we do not know something, we say that we do not know it. If a famous claim turns out to be exaggerated — and this happens often with viral stories — we explain what is actually true, even when the truth is less exciting than the rumour. A smaller true story is worth more than a big false one.</p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>News changes quickly, and even with careful checking, mistakes can happen. When we find a mistake — or when a reader finds one and tells us — we correct the post and mark it as updated, so readers can see that it changed. If you spot anything wrong on this website, please email us at <a href="mailto:lartkapagal@gmail.com">lartkapagal@gmail.com</a> and tell us which post it is in. Genuine corrections are always welcome, and we are grateful to the readers who send them.</p>

    <h2>Who runs Talha M Blog?</h2>
    <p>Talha M Blog is run by Talha M, an independent publisher from Pakistan. This is a small, independent website — it is not part of any large media group, political party or company. Topics are chosen because they are important, interesting or useful for ordinary readers, never because someone paid for them to appear. The website is supported by advertising, which is described in our <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>. Advertisements never decide what we write.</p>

    <h2>Why simple English?</h2>
    <p>Most of the world's people do not speak English as their first language, yet most of the world's news is published in English first. Students preparing for exams, young people looking for their first job, freelancers working with foreign clients, and millions of ordinary readers in Pakistan, India, Bangladesh, Africa and many other regions read the news in their second or third language. Writing simply is not writing less — it is writing with more care. If a story cannot be explained in simple words, it usually means the writer has not understood it fully himself.</p>

    <h2>Contact us</h2>
    <p>For questions, feedback, story suggestions or corrections, visit our <a href="/contact/">Contact page</a> or email <a href="mailto:lartkapagal@gmail.com">lartkapagal@gmail.com</a>. We read every genuine message. Thank you for reading Talha M Blog — we hope you leave every story understanding a little more than when you arrived.</p>

